ed}8 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(v)=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix $T$ of $T?$\n\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067709/state.json" "file_sha256" "32d3b2aaea45ef2c761901fa9d0371783468af5a81145aa14a60db30fef590af"} {"question" "q-328230" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can write $T(v)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $T(v)=\\frac{3}{4}v,$ and so the linear transformation $T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nThe matrix that represents a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067709/state.json" "file_sha256" "32d3b2aaea45ef2c761901fa9d0371783468af5a81145aa14a60db30fef590af"} {"question" "q-328230" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067709/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "66a4a2cd7a87fed0565d1e2c0c5138123ab823a63b162a625de9fcb3d7f73c34"} {"question" "q-328230" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067709/state.json" "file_sha256" "32d3b2aaea45ef2c761901fa9d0371783468af5a81145aa14a60db30fef590af"} {"q